# UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer exceptions for the weekly sanitizer run (ci.yml).
# Each entry is one kind of check in one function, with the reason. Keep the
# list short: everything not listed here stays fully checked.

# LAME 3.100 (external/lame, unchanged third-party code; no newer release
# exists). frame_id_matches() in libmp3lame/id3tag.c slides a 0xff mask across
# a signed int, and the last step (0xff0000 << 8) overflows it. GCC, Clang and
# MSVC all give the intended 0xff000000, so the right ID3 frames are chosen.
# The self-test reads the MP3 tags back on every build (Linux, Windows, macOS)
# to prove it ("MP3 tags read back exactly").
shift-base:frame_id_matches

# setLameTagFrameHeader() in libmp3lame/VbrTag.c builds each field of the LAME
# tag's frame header with a mask made as ~(-1 << n), a shift of a negative
# int. Every compiler gives the intended mask of n ones. The self-test exports
# MP3s that players read back at the right rate and length, which needs that
# header ("MP3 export plays back", "MP3 of a 96 kHz mix").
shift-base:setLameTagFrameHeader

# putbits2() in libmp3lame/bitstream.c writes bits into a byte as
# (val >> j) << bit_index in a signed int, which overflows for a wide val. Only
# the low eight bits are kept (the byte), and those are the same on every
# compiler, so the stream is right: the self-test's MP3s decode with the level
# they were made at.
shift-base:putbits2

# LAME needs three entries here now, all the same signed-shift idiom. Revisit
# by patching those shifts to unsigned arithmetic (or by replacing LAME) if it
# needs another, or if AddressSanitizer ever finds a memory error in it.
